    {
      "sample_index": 6,
      "text": "To determine the only integer whose square is less than its double, let's denote the integer by \\( x \\). We need to find \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\[ x^2 < 2x \\]\n\nFirst, rearrange the inequality to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 2x < 0 \\]\n\nNext, factor the left-hand side:\n\n\\[ x(x - 2) < 0 \\]\n\nTo solve this inequality, we need to determine the intervals where the product \\( x(x - 2) \\) is negative. The roots of the equation \\( x(x - 2) = 0 \\) are \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\). These roots divide the number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, 0) \\), \\( (0, 2) \\), and \\( (2, \\infty) \\).\n\nWe test a point in each interval to determine the sign of \\( x(x - 2) \\):\n\n1. For \\( x \\in (-\\infty, 0) \\), choose \\( x = -1 \\):\n   \\[ (-1)(-1 - 2) = (-1)(-3) = 3 \\quad (\\text{positive}) \\]\n\n2. For \\( x \\in (0, 2) \\), choose \\( x = 1 \\):\n   \\[ (1)(1 - 2) = (1)(-1) = -1 \\quad (\\text{negative}) \\]\n\n3. For \\( x \\in (2, \\infty) \\), choose \\( x = 3 \\):\n   \\[ (3)(3 - 2) = (3)(1) = 3 \\quad (\\text{positive}) \\]\n\nFrom the tests, we see that \\( x(x - 2) \\) is negative only in the interval \\( (0, 2) \\). Therefore, the only integer \\( x \\) that satisfies the inequality \\( x(x - 2) < 0 \\) is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nTo verify, we check:\n\n\\[ 1^2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\]\n\nSince \\( 1 < 2 \\), the integer \\( x = 1 \\) satisfies the original condition. Therefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
