      "text": "Let's denote the integer by \\( x \\). We need to find the integer \\( x \\) such that its square is less than its double. This can be expressed as the inequality:\n\\[ x^2 < 2x \\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the inequality to bring all terms to one side:\n\\[ x^2 - 2x < 0 \\]\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side:\n\\[ x(x - 2) < 0 \\]\n\nThis inequality holds when the product of \\( x \\) and \\( x - 2 \\) is negative. To determine the intervals where this product is negative, we find the roots of the equation \\( x(x - 2) = 0 \\), which are \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\). These roots divide the number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, 0) \\), \\( (0, 2) \\), and \\( (2, \\infty) \\).\n\nWe test a point from each interval to determine where the product is negative:\n- For \\( x \\in (-\\infty, 0) \\), choose \\( x = -1 \\):\n  \\[ (-1)(-1 - 2) = (-1)(-3) = 3 > 0 \\]\n  So, the product is positive in this interval.\n\n- For \\( x \\in (0, 2) \\), choose \\( x = 1 \\):\n  \\[ (1)(1 - 2) = (1)(-1) = -1 < 0 \\]\n  So, the product is negative in this interval.\n\n- For \\( x \\in (2, \\infty) \\), choose \\( x = 3 \\):\n  \\[ (3)(3 - 2) = (3)(1) = 3 > 0 \\]\n  So, the product is positive in this interval.\n\nTherefore, the inequality \\( x(x - 2) < 0 \\) is satisfied in the interval \\( (0, 2) \\). Since \\( x \\) must be an integer, the only integer in this interval is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nThus, the only integer whose square is less than its double is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
